===Verb===
{{es-verb}}

# to [[be able]], [[can]]
#: {{ux|es|¿'''Puedes''' acompañarme mañana?|'''Could you''' come with me tomorrow?}}
#: {{ux|es|No '''puedes''' salir ya que no tienes permiso.|'''You can''''t go out since you're not allowed.}}
#: {{ux|es|No '''puedo''' hablar francés.|'''I can''''t speak French.}}
#: {{ux|es|'''Podría''' ayudar si me dejaran.|I could help you if you would let me.}}
# {{lb|es|El Salvador|colloquial|reflexive}} to be [[familiar]] with something, or with somebody's personality
#: {{ux|es|¿'''Te podés''' el número de tu prima?|'''Do you know''' your cousin's phone number?}}
#: {{ux|es|Como entrenador, tengo que '''poderme''' bien a todos mis jugadores.|As a coach, I must '''know''' my players really well.}}

====Usage notes====
This verb is replaced with ''[[saber]]'' or ''ser [[capaz]] de'' for use with skills (as in to know how to do something) and omitted when relating to verbs of sense.
* "You can do it." – ''Puedes hacerlo.'' (uses ''poder'')
* "She can swim." – ''Ella es capaz de nadar.'' (uses ''ser capaz de'')
* "I can see it." – ''Lo [[ver|veo]].'' (omitted)

In the [[preterite]], ''poder'' means "to succeed in doing."
* {{ux|es|Pude hablar con el profesor después de la clase.|I managed to speak with the professor after class.}}
* {{ux|es|Podíamos hablar con el profesor cuando quisiéramos.|We could talk to the professor whenever we wanted.}}

